<!--
The shapes of types depend neither on the order in which they are scanned nor on each other's scans
(opcua/instance-coverage).
- SType has a mandatory object G and a mandatory placeholder <Item>. TType, its subtype, adds only an optional
  BaseObjectType object, which gives no rule. It keeps a shape of its own, in which its conforming Item1 is found.
- UType, which only an Organizes reference reaches, declares a mandatory child Tc of TType and needs its rule: the
  instance lacks Tc.
- VType overrides its supertype's child G with a declaration that adds only an object without rules; V2Type, its
  subtype, adds a mandatory variable. No relationship may refer to a shape that does not exist.
- MType (no member of its own, subtype of NType) and WType, which declares a mandatory child of NType, are only
  organized, and no NType object exists. NType's shape comes from WType's scan and covers MType: the missing Need is
  reported once.
-->

